# DIAYN's settings.
#
#     cp example.env .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# then fill in the two blanks just below, and run
# `.venv/bin/python diayn.py setup`.
# Everything after them is optional: each line is commented out and shows the
# code's own default, or an example where no default would do. Uncomment only
# what you change.
#
# How it loads: from POLLER_ENV_FILE if that is set, else from .env beside
# diayn.py; never from the working directory. A variable already in the
# process environment (pm2's env block, a systemd Environment= line, a shell
# export) wins over this file. An empty value keeps the default. Settings are
# read once, when a command starts: restart the bot after an edit.
# `.venv/bin/python diayn.py config` prints what a run would use, shows the
# token and the Gemini key only as set or not set, and counts the owners' ids
# rather than printing them.

# ── Fill these in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your bot's token: in the Discord developer portal, your application, then
# Bot, then Reset Token. Anyone with it can act as your bot, so this file stays
# out of git (.gitignore sees to it) and at mode 600.
DISCORD_TOKEN=
# Sent in the User-Agent of every request to a job board, so a board's owner
# can reach whoever runs this rather than block it: a project URL or a role
# mailbox, such as https://example.org/internships or mailto:jobs@example.org,
# never a personal address.
POLL_CONTACT=

# ── Who runs the bot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The bot's owners, who may use it and grant others access: Discord user ids,
# separated by commas. Empty means the Discord application's owner, or the
# members of the team that owns it.
# DIAYN_OWNER_IDS=
# The time zone for alert hours and the bot's daily housekeeping, named
# wherever the bot shows an hour, such as America/New_York or Europe/London.
# DIAYN_TZ=UTC

# ── Gemini: the fit check ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional, and recommended. With a key, before an alert is sent, Gemini checks
# whether each role the rule-based matcher picked suits that person, leaves out
# the ones that do not, and says why for the rest. Without one, nothing is sent
# to Gemini and alerts carry the rule-based matches unchecked. What it sends:
# each person's profile labels and each role's title, company, location and
# term, never a resume, a name or a Discord id (README, Privacy). Each person
# can turn it off. A key comes from Google AI Studio.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# The model, shared with --llm below. Match the limits to your AI Studio
# dashboard. Or gemini-3.6-flash.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# Roles checked per request.
# FIT_BATCH=15
# Requests a day, and a minute. These, plus GEMINI_RPD and GEMINI_RPM below
# for --llm, must fit within the key's quota.
# FIT_RPD=200
# FIT_RPM=10
# Tries per request, the first included, before the fit check sends its alert
# unchecked and --llm falls back to the regular expressions.
# GEMINI_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# Seconds one request may take (0 means no deadline).
# GEMINI_HTTP_TIMEOUT=150
# The quota day: Gemini's free tier resets its daily cap at midnight here, and
# both budgets count their day in this zone. Separate from DIAYN_TZ.
# LLM_DAY_TZ=America/Los_Angeles

# ── Gemini: --llm ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only for `run --llm`, `sweep --llm` and the like, which classify new postings
# by title with Gemini instead of the regular expressions. Off unless asked for.
# Postings classified per request.
# GEMINI_BATCH=25
# The defaults are the stricter flash tier's (5 RPM / 250 RPD), so switching
# models cannot exceed a limit by surprise. flash-lite's free tier allows 15
# RPM and 500 RPD: raise these for it only after checking the dashboard.
# GEMINI_RPM=5
# GEMINI_RPD=250
# GEMINI_TPM=250000
# Batches in a row that fail every try before the run stops calling Gemini.
# GEMINI_MAX_BATCH_FAILURES=3

# ── Where the data lives ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default: data/ in the checkout, which git ignores and an upgrade leaves
# alone. It holds postings.db, the scraper's ledger, and users.db, the bot's
# own, with everyone's profile; `setup` makes it at mode 700. Paths must be
# absolute: a relative one, or one starting with ~, which a .env does not
# expand, stops the start, since it would be taken from the working directory.
#
# The data directory. users.db is always in it; the three files below are too,
# unless named on their own.
# DIAYN_DATA=/home/<user>/diayn-data
#
# Only to keep one file outside DIAYN_DATA. The sweeper lock is POSTINGS_DB
# plus ".lock", and the bot reads the same path.
# POSTINGS_DB=/home/<user>/diayn-data/postings.db
# The boards `discover` found. Read at every start; `discover` rewrites it
# whole, over any hand edits.
# BOARDS_FILE=/home/<user>/diayn-data/boards.json
# Written by `discover --yc`, so a second run skips companies already probed.
# YC_CACHE=/home/<user>/diayn-data/yc_cache.json

# ── Politeness ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At most this many requests in flight to any one job board's host (at least 1).
# POLL_HOST_CONCURRENCY=4
# Seconds between the starts of two requests to one host (0 or more).
# POLL_HOST_MIN_INTERVAL=0.12

# ── Where this file is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read from the environment before any .env loads, because it names the .env:
# set it in pm2's env block, a systemd Environment= line or the shell, never
# here. Empty means the .env beside diayn.py. Naming a file that does not exist
# stops the start.
# POLLER_ENV_FILE=
